# ADR-2607161852: kotoba-lang/dtn gains a durable (disk-backed) store and HMAC bundle integrity

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-16
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki

## Problem

ADR-2607161817 gave `kotoba-lang/dtn` a real internet-overlay TCP transport
with genuine cross-process delivery and in-memory store-and-forward. Asked
directly *「残りの gap は?」* after that landed, the orchestrating session
enumerated the remaining gaps in two categories: environment-blocked
(mesh-radio/satellite hardware, real carrier gateway credentials, a new
cloud-itonami actor — none fixable by writing more code here) and
still-code-fixable (in-memory-only store, zero transport authentication,
no multi-hop routing, no peer discovery, no NAT traversal, remaining
RFC 9171 wire-format details).

Of the code-fixable list, two items directly undercut the design's own
stated top motivation (disaster/carrier-outage resilience, from
ADR-2607161743's clarifying questions): a node that loses every queued
message on restart isn't meaningfully resilient, and a transport that
accepts unauthenticated bundles from anyone claiming any source E.164
isn't meaningfully trustworthy. This ADR closes those two.

## Decision

### Decision 1: durable store — `kotoba.dtn.store`, an append-only EDN log

`src/kotoba/dtn/store.cljc` adds pure (`.cljc`, testable under
`clojure -M:test`) serialize/deserialize/compact functions for an
append-only, newline-delimited `pr-str` EDN log — one bundle per line, the
same \"simple, append-only, human-diffable\" shape this workspace already
uses for its own ledgers (`canvas-ledger.edn`, `fleet-db.ledger.edn`).
`deserialize-line` returns nil on a blank/malformed line rather than
throwing, so a partially-written last line from a crash mid-write doesn't
corrupt the whole log on reload. The actual file I/O (`node:fs`) is Node-only,
behind a reader-conditional, matching this repo's established `.cljc`
core + `.cljs`-only-I/O split.

`kotoba.dtn.transport.tcp/start-node!` gained an **optional** `:store-path`.
When passed: on startup, the on-disk log is loaded into the in-memory
`:store`; every bundle that falls back to `:store` also gets appended to
disk; every successful `retry-store!` pass rewrites the compacted
(delivered/expired bundles dropped) set back to disk. When omitted,
behavior is byte-identical to before (in-memory only) — not a breaking
change to the existing demo/CLI callers.

### Decision 2: bundle integrity — `kotoba.dtn.auth`, pre-shared-secret HMAC-SHA256

`src/kotoba/dtn/auth.cljc` adds `sign-bundle`/`verify-bundle` over a
per-peer pre-shared secret (HMAC-SHA256, `javax.crypto.Mac` on `:clj` /
`node:crypto` on `:cljs` — same portable-core pattern as Decision 1).
`kotoba.dtn.transport.tcp/start-node!` gained an **optional**
`:peer-secrets` map; when a secret is configured for a peer, outbound
bundles to it are signed and inbound bundles from it are verified before
being admitted to `:inbox` — a bundle that fails verification is logged and
**dropped, not stored/retried** (a forged/tampered bundle is a security
event, not a legitimate delivery worth retrying). Omitted, behavior is
unchanged from before.

This is deliberately scoped as **pre-shared-secret origin/tamper checking
for a small configured peer set**, not a PKI, not TLS, and explicitly
**not replay-protected** (no nonce/sequence dedup) — stated plainly in both
the namespace docstring and the README, following this repo's existing
\"claim exactly what you built\" discipline (the same discipline
`kotoba.dtn.router`'s docstring already uses to disclaim being full CGR).
A PKI/certificate system and replay protection remain undone, named
explicitly rather than silently scoped in or out.

### Decision 3: verify with real re-runs, not the implementing agent's self-report alone

The demo (`test/kotoba/dtn/transport/tcp_demo.cljs`) gained two more
scenarios: 4a actually stops a node, \"restarts\" it (fresh `start-node!`
against the same `:store-path`), and asserts the reloaded in-memory store
contains the bundle that was there before — proving the persistence
round-trip against the real file, not just the pure serialize/deserialize
functions in isolation. 4b sends one legitimately-signed message (accepted)
and one bundle signed with the wrong secret over a raw socket, bypassing
`send-message!`'s normal signing path (rejected) — proving the transport
layer actually enforces verification, not just that `verify-bundle` returns
the right boolean in a unit test.

As with ADR-2607161817, this was independently re-verified by the
orchestrating session from a cold clone before writing this ADR, not
trusted from the implementing agent's report alone.

## Verification

- `clojure -M:test` → 26 tests / 112 assertions (was 84; +28 new), 0
  failures, 0 errors. Independently re-run from a cold clone.
- `clojure -M:lint` → 0 errors, 0 warnings. Independently re-run.
- `nbb --classpath \"src:test:../phone/src:../html/src:../css/src\" test/kotoba/dtn/transport/tcp_demo.cljs` → `RESULT: 5/5 scenarios passed`, exit 0 (scenarios 1-3 from ADR-2607161817 unmodified and still passing; new 4a/4b both pass). Independently re-run from a cold clone, output matches the implementing agent's report exactly.
- Pushed to `github.com/kotoba-lang/dtn` (public), commit `a89edf50cfcc7083f42c2a0446c9e0483a1f6a06`.
- `manifest/west.yml`: pin advanced via `--entry dtn` (1-line diff, `fdd2b4480f0d→a89edf50cfcc`, `verify-west-pins: 1 件の pin 変更をすべて検証 OK`), landed via an isolated sibling-path worktree + GitHub API server-side merge, matching the two prior ADRs' own landing procedure.

## Consequences

- The two gaps most directly undercutting this design's own resilience
  premise — volatile store, unauthenticated transport — are closed with
  real, re-verified code. A node process can now crash and recover its
  undelivered queue; a peer can no longer inject bundles under a forged
  source identity without the shared secret.
- Both additions are additive/optional (`:store-path`, `:peer-secrets`) —
  zero behavior change for existing callers that don't opt in, so
  ADR-2607161817's own demo scenarios 1-3 needed no modification.
- Still explicitly undone, unchanged from the prior gap inventory: no PKI
  (pre-shared-secret only), no replay protection, no multi-hop routing, no
  peer discovery (`kotoba-lang/net` gossip composition), no NAT traversal
  (`kotoba-lang/turn` composition), remaining RFC 9171 wire-format details
  (CBOR, BPSec, fragmentation), mesh-radio/satellite hardware, real carrier
  gateway credentials, and a consuming cloud-itonami actor. None of these
  are silently claimed as done.
- A durable log that is only compacted on a successful `retry-store!` pass
  can grow between passes under sustained undeliverable-message load — an
  known, documented characteristic, not a claimed-away one; no size cap or
  rotation was added in this ADR.

## Alternatives considered

- **A real embedded database (SQLite, LMDB) instead of an append-only EDN
  log for the durable store.** Rejected for this scope: the append-only-log
  shape already matches this workspace's own ledger convention, needs zero
  new dependencies (still `node:fs` only), and the demo's correctness bar
  (survive-a-restart) doesn't need transactional query semantics a real DB
  would add complexity for without benefit here.
- **A full PKI / certificate-issuance system for bundle authentication.**
  Rejected: this ADR series has already deferred running-server/
  key-management-operations concerns to a future consuming actor (per
  ADR-2607161743 Decision 5); pre-shared-secret HMAC is the honest minimum
  that closes \"can anyone forge a source identity\" without building an
  entire CA this scope doesn't need.
- **Adding replay-attack protection (nonce/sequence dedup) in the same
  pass.** Rejected for this ADR specifically to keep the two closed gaps
  cleanly verifiable and scoped; named explicitly as still-undone rather
  than bundled in partially-done.
- **Trusting the implementing agent's report without independent
  re-verification**, again. Rejected for the same reason as
  ADR-2607161817 — re-run from a cold clone before writing Verification.

